   CCoommppaattiibbiilliittyy ooff CCLLOONNEE__NNEEWWPPIIDD wwiitthh ootthheerr CCLLOONNEE__** ffllaaggss
       In current versions of  Linux,  CCLLOONNEE__NNEEWWPPIIDD  can’t  be  combined  with
       CCLLOONNEE__TTHHRREEAADD.   Threads  are  required  to be in the same PID namespace
       such that the threads in a process can  send  signals  to  each  other.
       Similarly,  it  must be possible to see all of the threads of a process
       in the pprroocc(5) filesystem.  Additionally, if two threads were  in  dif‐
       ferent  PID  namespaces, the process ID of the process sending a signal
       could not be meaningfully encoded when a signal is sent  (see  the  de‐
       scription  of  the _s_i_g_i_n_f_o___t type in ssiiggaaccttiioonn(2)).  Since this is com‐
       puted when a signal is enqueued, a signal queue shared by processes  in
       multiple PID namespaces would defeat that.

       In  earlier versions of Linux, CCLLOONNEE__NNEEWWPPIIDD was additionally disallowed
       (failing with the error EEIINNVVAALL) in combination with CCLLOONNEE__SSIIGGHHAANNDD  (be‐
       fore  Linux  4.3) as well as CCLLOONNEE__VVMM (before Linux 3.12).  The changes
       that lifted these restrictions have also been ported to earlier  stable
       kernels.

